### Production Worker Pool Solution
Cap maximum concurrent worker goroutines using buffered job channels and `context.Context` cancellation:

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

type Job struct {
	ID int
}

func Worker(ctx context.Context, id int, jobs <-chan Job, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
	defer wg.Done()
	for {
		select {
		case <-ctx.Done():
			return
		case job, ok := <-jobs:
			if !ok {
				return
			}
			fmt.Printf("Worker %d processing job %d\n", id, job.ID)
			time.Sleep(100 * time.Millisecond)
		}
	}
}
```

### Practice Problem Statement
Implement a concurrent token bucket rate limiter in Go that limits requests to a maximum rate of R tokens/sec with a burst capacity of B.

```go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"golang.org/x/time/rate"
	"time"
)

type RateLimiter struct {
	limiter *rate.Limiter
}

func NewRateLimiter(r rate.Limit, b int) *RateLimiter {
	return &RateLimiter{limiter: rate.NewLimiter(r, b)}
}

func (rl *RateLimiter) Allow(ctx context.Context) error {
	return rl.limiter.Wait(ctx)
}
```
